{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Offer sacrifices of righteousness</b> — these are the shelamim, for their offerings are not for the sake of an asham, or a hata'at, or an olah. <b>Upon the heart</b> — this is the thought. And there is no objection from <i>then You will desire sacrifices of righteousness, burnt offering and whole offering</i>, for the meaning is: and an olah corresponding to Reuben and Simeon." "And the meaning of <b>sacrifice</b> — abandon idle words and give thanks to HaShem, for He has given pleasantness to you."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The argument is compressed: Ibn Ezra explains that 'upon the heart' means the sacrifice is offered with proper intention. The citation of Reuben and Simeon is cryptic and left as-is."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:26:20.714025749Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}